\subsection{Cross-framework verification}

The core conclusion of this study---the existence of Silent Discordance and its laws---was not established by any single author alone, but was jointly confirmed by three authors using three completely independent tools (DMRG/exact diagonalization, Bethe ansatz/boundary CFT, TAT structural diagnostics) on the same set of public data. Rastislav Draho\v{s}'s physical verification and Marat Sultanov's structural diagnostics drew the same boundary from opposite directions.

\subsection{Experimental limitations}

In accordance with the meta-reflection principle, the following limitations must be explicitly declared: (1) Except for the fractal graph ($N=15$), all cross-graph scans were completed at $N=6$. (2) All experiments were completed on a single personal computer, with an upper bound of exact diagonalization at $N=15$. (3) All experiments used SU(2)-symmetric rules and their variants, with the Rashba SOC rule as a single extension. (4) All diagnostics were performed on the ground state. (5) The cyclic driving experiment was performed on a single system size ($N=6$) and a single rule (Heisenberg).

\subsection{Methodological Boundary and Open Questions}

In accordance with the meta-reflection principle, we explicitly state that the three laws and six morphologies of Silent Discordance have been verified only on finite systems ($N \le 15$). The survival of certain morphologies---in particular the LDS Valley on the fractal graph---in the thermodynamic limit remains an open question. Draho\v{s} pushed the tensor-RG toolchain to L3 on available hardware, where the valley depth did not converge; this is an honest convergence limit, not a model defect. The thermodynamic-limit verification is a separate, next-level research program that does not retroactively invalidate the finite-system discoveries. The precedent of quantum many-body scars---discovered on finite PXP chains and still under active investigation regarding their thermodynamic fate---illustrates that a phenomenon can be both real at finite sizes and an open question in the thermodynamic limit. We invite independent groups with access to larger-scale GPU resources to take up the convergence of the LDS Valley using the publicly available TRG toolchain.

\section{Conclusion}

We first discovered the Silent Discordance phenomenon in the PXP model, and independently replicated it in a self-built fractal-graph quantum system. Through a systematic scan over seven relation graphs, four generation rules, and extensions to Rashba SOC, non-equilibrium driving, current algebra models, and comprehensive parity analysis, we locked down six laws of Silent Discordance. The first three govern the necessary and sufficient conditions for its emergence; the latter three govern its behavior in dynamic and rule-extension dimensions. \textbf{The entire research program---from the first observation in the PXP model to the independent replication in the fractal graph, to the cross-graph cross-rule matrix, to the construction of the Golden Staff compiler, to the triple independent cross-framework verification---points to a single core: ``relation precedes entity'' is not a philosophical speculation, but a physical principle that can be compiled into code, tested in numerical experiments, and verified by independent methods.} Specifically, the sixth law (law of asymmetric driving necessity) unifies the shift experiments, the current algebra model, and the non-equilibrium results under a single principle: Silent Discordance requires asymmetric injection of contradiction to trigger abrupt reorganization. Symmetric driving leads to smooth adaptation and no valley.

The unique aspect of this study lies in its collaborative mode. Rastislav Draho\v{s}'s four critical corrections---from identifying variable confounding to $\chi\to\infty$ extrapolation---each prevented the publication of erroneous conclusions at key junctures, providing a second verification for every core discovery using independent codes and analytical methods. Marat Sultanov's TAT cross-framework blind tests and four demonstrations of honest silence---actively rejecting spurious signals when data are insufficient or not significant---proved that a diagnostic tool can know when to shut up. The two collaborators drew the same boundary from opposite directions: the Li-Draho\v{s}-Sultanov Valley is a real, non-sharp defect-mode signal, confirmed by independent replication and cross-framework cross-validation.

The discovery process of Silent Discordance demonstrates a scientific methodology centered on honesty, anchored on data, and constrained by predefined success criteria. Five negative experiments drew the map for the emergence of the positive signal. We strictly adhered to the principle of ``diagnose, not prescribe''---this paper only reports the existence of Silent Discordance and its necessary and sufficient conditions, and makes no assertion about its universality in other models beyond the scope supported by the current data.

All data, codes, and experimental protocols are publicly available for independent verification. Future research directions include: testing the finite-size scaling behavior of Silent Discordance at larger system sizes; using HOTRG methods to determine the survival of the LDS Valley in the thermodynamic limit; extending the Golden Staff compiler to higher spins and fermionic systems; and probing the memory frontier with cyclic driving protocols on larger systems and under Rashba SOC rules.

