\section{Appendix: Collaborative Methodology and Contribution Records}
\label{app:collaboration}

This appendix provides the detailed chronological record of the collaborative corrections by Rastislav Draho\v{s} and the TAT blind-test interventions by Marat Sultanov. These records constitute the methodological backbone of this study and demonstrate how the tool-rationality paradox was rigorously prevented at every key juncture.

\subsection{Timeline of Draho\v{s}'s critical corrections}

\textbf{Prediction 1 phase (testing the $A$--$C$ relation):} The initial goal of this study was to examine the relation between the spin-gap prefactor $A$ and the topological connectivity index $C$. Guanghao Li ran DMRG calculations on the Hubbard model ($U/t=4$, half-filling) and initially found a high $R^2$ correlation between $A$ and $C$. This preliminary finding was sent to Rastislav Draho\v{s} by email. After receiving the data, Draho\v{s} independently analyzed the raw CSV files and discovered that $C$ on a linear chain is a deterministic function of $L$ ($C \approx 0.9745 - 0.624/L$, $R^2=0.99999$), so the high correlation between $A$ and $C$ was a mathematical tautology of the scaling relation between $A$ and $L$, not a physical discovery. Draho\v{s} sent this correction to Li in early July 2026 by email, and Li publicly acknowledged the error the same day. This correction withdrew Prediction 1 from a wrong direction and repositioned it as the correct experimental scheme of fixing $L$ and scanning multiple topologies.

\textbf{Menu 1 phase (single-bond defect scan):} After correcting the misreading of the $A$--$C$ relation, Li carried out single-bond defect scanning experiments---fixing $L=40$ and $L=60$, and changing the hopping strength of the central bond from $0.5t$ to $1.5t$. The results showed that $A$ changed by over $500\%$ while $C$ changed by less than $1\%$. Li initially interpreted this as evidence of ``relation determines entity'' and sent the data to Draho\v{s}. After receiving the data, Draho\v{s} pointed out that the weak bond triggers the Kane-Fisher renormalization-group flow in the Mott spin sector, changing the effective scaling length $L_\text{eff}$ of the system, and the change in $A$ is a scaling effect, not new physics. Draho\v{s} simultaneously provided the exact Bethe ansatz value of the spin-wave velocity $\pi v_s$ as an external benchmark, and cited the original references of Eggert-Affleck (1992) and Kane-Fisher (1992). Li accepted this re-positioning.

\textbf{Menu 2 phase (periodic chain comparison):} Li then carried out a comparison experiment between periodic and open boundary conditions. Under periodic boundaries, the charge gap did not converge (marked as $-999$). Draho\v{s} used the exact Bethe ansatz to provide the analytical value of the Mott gap $\Delta_c(U=4)=1.286727$, demonstrating that this value is analytically determined and boundary-independent, thereby closing the gate condition. He then voluntarily corrected his own earlier confusion between the removal energy and the true Mott gap. Draho\v{s} also pointed out that the $\sim 15\%$ difference in $A$ values between periodic and open boundaries is a standard prediction of Cardy boundary CFT, not evidence of ``relation precedes entity''.

\textbf{Direction 2 phase (periodic chain with defect and $\chi\to\infty$ extrapolation):} Li placed a central weak-bond defect on a periodic chain and measured $A=0.47$ at $\chi=100$. After receiving this data, Draho\v{s} pointed out that a periodic boundary plus a weak bond is a classic hard case for finite MPS, and $A=0.47$ was very likely a numerical artifact. He carried out systematic $\chi\to\infty$ extrapolation ($\chi=100\to600$), corrected the value to the converged value $A=3.19\pm0.03$, and on this basis proposed the methodological warning that ``a low-$\chi$ periodic chain with a defect can silently produce an order-of-magnitude wrong energy gap''. Draho\v{s} also pointed out that the open-chain weak-bond value $A=5.47$ is a clean Kane-Fisher effect---a partial cut at $0.5t$ lies exactly on the path towards the fully cut fixed point.

